  "content": {
    "id": "p38-q22-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "22",
    "prompt": "Alice and Ben each toss a fair coin $n$ times. Show that the probability that they both get the same number of heads is $\\binom{2n}{n}\\frac{1}{2^{2n}}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\displaystyle\\binom{2n}{n}\\frac1{2^{2n}}$.\n\nReplace Ben’s heads by tails using fair-coin symmetry; equal counts then correspond to a total of $n$ successes.",
      "worked": "Let $A$ and $B$ count Alice’s and Ben’s heads. Since Ben’s coin is fair, $B$ and $n-B$ have the same distribution. Independence therefore gives\n$$\\Pr(A=B)=\\Pr(A=n-B)=\\Pr(A+B=n).$$\nTogether they make $2n$ independent fair-coin tosses, so $A+B$ has binomial distribution with $2n$ trials and success probability $\\frac12$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(A=B)&=\\binom{2n}{n}\\left(\\frac12\\right)^n\\left(\\frac12\\right)^n\\\\\\Pr(A=B)&=\\binom{2n}{n}\\frac1{2^{2n}}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 36
  },
